    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§168-b, 168-f & 168-q, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S5775\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act\nto amend\nthe correction law, in relation to requiring level two sex offenders to\nregister their employment address and relating to certain requirements\nof distribution of the subdirectory\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nThe bill will require all level two\nsex offenders in New York State to register the address of their\nemployment and to update certain sections of law with current practice.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection one amends paragraph (a) of\nthe subdivision 1 of section 168-b of the correction law to mandate\nthat level two sex offenders (along with level three) provide a place\nof actual or expected place of employment.\n\nSection two amends paragraph (b-1) of subdivision 2 of section 168-f\nof the correction law to verify whether or not a level two sex\noffenders address of employment remains the same or has changed since\nsuch employment was last reported.\n\nSection three amends subdivision 1 of section 168-·q to eliminate the\nrequirement that a subdirectory of level two and three sex offenders\nbe distributed to local law enforcement agencies and updated monthly.\n\nSection four is the effective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nTo increase the usefulness of the sex offender\nregistry, this bill will require the registration of the address of\nemployment for level two sex offenders.\n\nWhen SORA was first enacted, the subdirectory of level two and three\nsex offenders was printed and distributed to law enforcement agencies\nby the division of criminal justice services. Given the number of\noffenders listed on the subdirectory, it soon became impossible to\ncreate a printed subdirectory. Several years ago, the division of\ncriminal justice services discontinued print access and directed law\nenforcement agencies to allow the public to view the subdirectory\nthrough the DCJS website.\nHowever, the outdated language directing the annual distribution and\nmonthly updating remains in the law and should be removed to reflect\ncurrent practice.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\nMinimal.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n\n",
